{"subjectId":"c","id":"hash-table","title":"Hash Table","group":"Data Structures & Algorithms in C","order":45,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">An array of buckets indexed by <code>hash(key) % capacity</code>. With a good hash function and a reasonable load factor, lookup, insert and delete are O(1) on average.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\n#define NBUCKETS 8\n\ntypedef struct Entry {\n    char *key;\n    int value;\n    struct Entry *next;               /* separate chaining */\n} Entry;\n\ntypedef struct { Entry *buckets[NBUCKETS]; size_t count; } Map;\n\nunsigned long hash(const char *s) {   /* djb2 by Dan Bernstein */\n    unsigned long h = 5381;\n    for (; *s; s++) h = h * 33 + (unsigned char)*s;\n    return h;\n}\n\nvoid map_put(Map *m, const char *key, int value) {\n    size_t i = hash(key) % NBUCKETS;\n    for (Entry *e = m-&gt;buckets[i]; e; e = e-&gt;next)\n        if (strcmp(e-&gt;key, key) == 0) { e-&gt;value = value; return; }   /* update */\n    Entry *e = malloc(sizeof *e);\n    if (!e) exit(1);\n    size_t n = strlen(key) + 1;\n    e-&gt;key = malloc(n);\n    if (!e-&gt;key) exit(1);\n    memcpy(e-&gt;key, key, n);\n    e-&gt;value = value;\n    e-&gt;next = m-&gt;buckets[i];          /* insert at bucket head */\n    m-&gt;buckets[i] = e;\n    m-&gt;count++;\n}\n\nint *map_get(Map *m, const char *key) {\n    for (Entry *e = m-&gt;buckets[hash(key) % NBUCKETS]; e; e = e-&gt;next)\n        if (strcmp(e-&gt;key, key) == 0) return &amp;e-&gt;value;\n    return NULL;\n}\n\nvoid map_free(Map *m) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; NBUCKETS; i++)\n        for (Entry *e = m-&gt;buckets[i], *next; e; e = next) {\n            next = e-&gt;next;\n            free(e-&gt;key);\n            free(e);\n        }\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    Map m = {0};\n    const char *words[] = {\"the\", \"cat\", \"sat\", \"on\", \"the\", \"mat\", \"the\", \"cat\"};\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i++) {\n        int *c = map_get(&amp;m, words[i]);\n        if (c) (*c)++; else map_put(&amp;m, words[i], 1);\n    }\n    const char *probe[] = {\"the\", \"cat\", \"mat\", \"dog\"};\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {\n        int *c = map_get(&amp;m, probe[i]);\n        printf(\"%s: %d\\n\", probe[i], c ? *c : 0);\n    }\n    printf(\"distinct words: %zu\\n\", m.count);\n    map_free(&amp;m);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>the: 3\ncat: 2\nmat: 1\ndog: 0\ndistinct words: 5</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Collision strategy</th><th>How</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Separate chaining</td><td>Each bucket is a linked list</td><td>Simple; degrades gracefully; extra pointers</td></tr><tr><td>Linear probing</td><td>Try <code>i + 1</code>, <code>i + 2</code>, … in the array</td><td>Cache-friendly; needs tombstones for delete; clustering</td></tr><tr><td>Quadratic / double hashing</td><td>Probe with a step that grows or comes from a 2nd hash</td><td>Less clustering</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Keep the <em>load factor</em> (<code>count / capacity</code>) below ~0.75 by resizing (allocate a bigger bucket array, rehash every entry). Resizing by doubling keeps insertion amortised O(1).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What makes a good hash function?</summary><div><p>It is fast, deterministic, and spreads typical keys uniformly across buckets so that small changes in the key change many output bits. For strings, FNV-1a, djb2 or xxHash/SipHash (the latter resists hash-flooding attacks) are common choices.</p></div></details>"}